0. PREAMBLE

This licence (the "Licence") is an agreement between you — an individual or a
single legal entity — and Mathis Demory (the "Licensor").

It covers Fouine (the "Software"): the application, the command line, the
background agent, the MCP server, the source code, the documentation, and
anything delivered with them or provided as support.

The Software is licensed, not sold. By installing, copying, compiling or
otherwise using it, you accept the Licence. If you do not accept it, do not
install or use the Software.

The Licence is the entire agreement between you and the Licensor concerning
the Software; it supersedes any prior proposal or understanding. The Software
is protected by copyright law and by international treaties.

1. GRANT OF LICENCE

(a) Installation and use. The Licensor grants you the right to install and
    use copies of the Software obtained from the Licensor — the disk image
    published by the Licensor, the official Homebrew cask, the built-in
    update — under the terms of your purchase.

(b) One licence per person, up to three Macs. The licence is granted to one
    person, who may install and activate the Software on up to three Macs of
    the same household. It is not shared between several people. You may
    release one of those activations at any time to use another Mac.

(c) Trial. The Software may be used without a licence key for a trial period
    of thirty days. When that period ends, updating the index stops and a
    licence key is required to resume it; searching, reading and exporting
    what is already indexed continue to work.

(d) Backup copies. You may make the copies of the Software necessary for
    backup and archival purposes.

2. OTHER RIGHTS AND LIMITATIONS

(a) Notices. You must not remove or alter any copyright, licence or
    attribution notice on any copy of the Software.

(b) No distribution. You must not distribute the Software to a third party:
    neither the source code, modified or not, nor a binary, nor a copy of the
    application, by any means, free of charge or for a fee.

(c) No rental or lending. You must not rent, lease or lend the Software, make
    it available to a third party, or offer it as a service.

(d) Licence verification. You must not circumvent, disable or alter the means
    by which the Software verifies your licence, nor help a third party do so.

(e) Support. The Licensor may provide support services for the Software. Any
    code supplied as part of them is part of the Software and is governed by
    the Licence.

(f) Compliance with applicable laws. You must comply with applicable law in
    your use of the Software.

3. SOURCE CODE

(a) The source code of the Software is available. You may read it, compile it
    and modify it for your own personal purpose, or to propose a contribution
    to the Software.

(b) A binary you compile yourself is governed by the Licence. You must not
    distribute it, sell it, or make it available to a third party, modified
    or not.

(c) The availability of the source code grants no other permission. In
    particular, it does not allow publishing a derived version, a
    redistribution, or a service based on the Software.

4. CONTRIBUTIONS

(a) By proposing a contribution to the Software — code, documentation,
    translation, fix, in any form — you grant the Licensor a perpetual,
    worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free, irrevocable and transferable
    licence, with the right to sublicense, to use, reproduce, modify, adapt,
    publish, translate and redistribute that contribution under any licence,
    including a commercial one.

(b) You warrant that you are the author of your contribution, or that you
    hold the rights needed to propose it on these terms, and that supplying
    it infringes no third-party right.

(c) You keep your rights in your contribution. There is no assignment to
    sign, and no separate contributor agreement.

5. THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

(a) The Software redistributes third-party components, which remain governed
    by their own licences. Their full notices are in
    THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md, delivered with the Software.

(b) The directory Sources/FouineMCPKit — and it alone — is under the MIT
    licence (Sources/FouineMCPKit/LICENSE). Those files may be reused under
    the MIT terms, including in another project. The shipped `fouine`
    executable, which links the rest of the Software, is governed by this
    Licence.

(c) Which licence covers which part of the project is set out in
    LICENSING.md.

6. TERMINATION

Without prejudice to its other rights, the Licensor may terminate the Licence
if you fail to comply with its terms. You must then stop using the Software
and destroy the copies in your possession. Articles 4, 7, 8, 9 and 10 survive
termination.

7. OWNERSHIP

The Software and all copies of it remain the property of the Licensor and, as
the case may be, of its suppliers. All rights not expressly granted by the
Licence are reserved.

The Software reads and indexes your documents: those remain yours. The
Licence grants the Licensor no right over them and no access to them.

8. NO WARRANTY

To the extent permitted by law, the Software is provided "as is", without
warranty of any kind, express or implied, including merchantability, fitness
for a particular purpose and non-infringement. The Licensor does not warrant
the accuracy or completeness of search results and of extracted or recognised
text, nor uninterrupted or error-free operation.

This article does not apply to the statutory warranties owed to consumers,
which are reserved by article 9.

9. LIMITATION OF LIABILITY

To the extent permitted by law, the Licensor is not liable for indirect,
special or incidental damages — loss of data, loss of use, loss of profit,
business interruption — arising out of the use of or the inability to use the
Software, even if advised of the possibility of such damages.

Nothing in the Licence excludes or limits the statutory warranty of
conformity (articles L. 217-3 et seq. of the French Consumer Code), the
warranty against hidden defects (articles 1641 et seq. of the French Civil
Code), or the Licensor's liability for wilful misconduct, gross negligence or
personal injury.

The Software is not a backup: keep your original documents.

10. GOVERNING LAW

The Licence is governed by French law. Failing an amicable settlement, the
French courts have jurisdiction. If you contract as a consumer, the mandatory
protective and jurisdictional rules of your country of residence continue to
apply.

